#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:1
msgid "Several documents in one run"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:3
msgid ""
"*Implemented.* Giving `-i` more than once, or naming a directory, is not "
"a loop over single-document runs. It is **one run with more pages in it**"
" — the documents are a *bundle*, and the engine treats the bundle the way"
" it would treat one large document that happened to be bound in several "
"volumes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:7
msgid "Why a report arrives as several files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:9
msgid ""
"The obvious reason to pass several documents is that you have many "
"reports of the same layout and want them all extracted. That case works, "
"but it is the uninteresting one: it would work just as well as several "
"separate runs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:13
msgid ""
"The reason the feature exists is the other one. **A single financial "
"report is often published as several files**, because its sections are "
"addressed to different audiences and are printed, dated and distributed "
"separately — the holdings schedule for one readership, the management and"
" governance sections for another, an annex for a regulator. Nothing about"
" that split follows the structure of the information; it follows the "
"structure of the publisher's obligations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:19
msgid ""
"The consequence is that **no one file contains what you need**. A "
"concrete shape of it, and a common one:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md
msgid "Wanted"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md
msgid "Where it is"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md
msgid "the investments"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md
msgid "document A"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md
msgid "the investment managers of those funds"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md
msgid "document B"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md
msgid "the asset managers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md
msgid "back in document A"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:28
msgid ""
"Extracting the investments *with their managers attached* is then not "
"possible from either file alone. Run separately, document A yields "
"holdings whose manager column is empty and a manager table with nothing "
"to attach it to; document B yields managers belonging to funds it never "
"mentions. Joining the two afterwards means reimplementing, outside the "
"engine and by hand, the matching the engine already does — on names that "
"were normalised inside it and are no longer normalised in the CSV."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:35
msgid "Passed together, the two files are one report:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:41
msgid "What \"as if it were one document\" means precisely"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:43
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"It means the **schedule** — the sequence of steps, where each step's "
"results are the next step's input filter ({doc}`schedule`) — runs once, "
"over the pages of the whole bundle:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:46
msgid "every document is classified on its own, so each page gets a class;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:47
msgid "the classified pages of all documents go into **one** pool;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:48
msgid ""
"step 1 runs over every page of that pool, whichever file it came from, "
"and its results become the filter data of step 2;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:50
msgid "step 2 runs over every page of the pool, again regardless of file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:52
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"So in the example above, the step that reads the investment managers out "
"of document B produces results that the later step over document A's "
"holdings pages **receives as its input filter**. That is the whole "
"mechanism, and it is why the bundle yields information neither file "
"yields alone. The same applies to promises ({doc}`promises`): a value "
"promised by a table in one document can be supplied by a page in another,"
" because resolution happens once, over everything."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:58
msgid ""
"Fund deduplication is also once, across the bundle — a fund named in both"
" documents is one row, not two."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:61
msgid "The rule: classify per document, schedule over the union"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:62
msgid "**Classification is per document. The schedule is over the union.**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:64
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Each document is opened and classified on its own, so a format's "
"finalizer sees one document's page sequence ({doc}`classification`). The "
"classified pages of every document are then poured into **one** pool, and"
" the schedule processes them together, caring only about the class each "
"page was given."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:68
msgid ""
"For everything downstream, having several documents changes **nothing** "
"except that each page carries the information of which document it came "
"from."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:71
msgid "Why that is the right shape"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:72
msgid "The two halves of the rule are each forced, for opposite reasons."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:74
msgid ""
"**Classification must be per document** because a continuation rule is a "
"fact about *adjacent pages of one file*. Over a union, page 1 of document"
" B follows page 400 of document A, and \"this page continues the previous"
" table\" would join tables that have nothing to do with each other."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:78
msgid ""
"**The schedule must be over the union** because that is what makes "
"several documents *one* extraction rather than several concatenated ones."
" Promise resolution and fund deduplication happen once, across everything"
" — so a fund named on the cover of document A can supply a value promised"
" by a table in document B, and the same fund appearing in both is one "
"row, not two."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:83
msgid "This is the property that makes the two commands genuinely different:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:90
msgid "Identifying a document"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:92
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Every output row carries a **`Report`** column naming the document it "
"came from, and the name comes from the document specifier "
"({doc}`../../guides/user/documents`):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md
msgid "Given"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md
msgid "Name"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md
msgid "`report.pdf`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md
msgid "the absolute path"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md
msgid "`report.pdf:EURIZON 2023`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md
msgid "`EURIZON 2023`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md
msgid "`https://…/r.pdf`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md
msgid "the URL"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md
msgid "a directory named `2024`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md
msgid "`2024/<file name>` per document in it"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:102
msgid "What the `Report` column replaced"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:103
msgid ""
"The `Report` column exists in **every** run, batch or not, and that "
"uniformity is the point of the design. Before it, batch mode had a "
"`prefix out` column whose job was to give a document a name, and non-"
"batch runs had no notion of naming at all — so the output schema depended"
" on which mode produced it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:108
msgid ""
"Making the name a property of the *document specifier* rather than of "
"*batch mode* removed the column, removed the special case, and made one "
"schema serve both. A consumer of `investments.csv` no longer has to know "
"how the run was invoked."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:112
msgid "How this differs from batch mode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:113
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Several documents in one invocation is not the same as {doc}`batch mode "
"<../../guides/user/advanced/batch>`, and the difference is exactly the "
"one this page is about. Several `-i` is **one job** over several "
"documents: one schedule, one pool of pages, shared filter data, shared "
"promises, shared deduplication. Batch is **one job per row**, each with "
"its own format and lists, each with its own schedule that sees nothing of"
" the others — which is precisely what you do *not* want for a report "
"split across files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/multidocument.md:119
msgid ""
"A rule of thumb: if the files are pieces of one report, they belong to "
"one job, several `-i`. If they are separate reports that merely happen to"
" be extracted at the same time, they belong to separate rows of a batch. "
"Batch rows can themselves name several documents, so a batch of bundles "
"composes naturally."
msgstr ""

